    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni",
      "body": "If you have learned Indian music, you know sargam: **Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni**. You can turn on sargam names in Settings to see them next to the letters.\n\nThis lesson is optional. Skip it if sargam isn't for you.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        60,
        62,
        64,
        65,
        67,
        69,
        71,
        72
      ],
      "labels": {
        "60": "Sa",
        "62": "Re",
        "64": "Ga",
        "65": "Ma",
        "67": "Pa",
        "69": "Dha",
        "71": "Ni",
        "72": "Sa"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Sa moves",
      "body": "Letter names are fixed: C is always C. Sa isn't. Sa is the home note of whatever you're playing, the way a singer picks their Sa.\n\nWith Sa on C, the white keys from C up are Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni, and then Sa again.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        60,
        62,
        64,
        65,
        67,
        69,
        71,
        72
      ],
      "labels": {
        "60": "Sa",
        "62": "Re",
        "64": "Ga",
        "65": "Ma",
        "67": "Pa",
        "69": "Dha",
        "71": "Ni",
        "72": "Sa"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "Sa on C",
      "body": "Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni Sa, on the white keys from C4 to C5.",
      "highlightMidi": [
